\paragraph{Horizontal drift and directional regularization}
\label{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}
After attitude compensation, small tilt errors leak gravity into horizontal
acceleration approximately as
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\vct\varepsilon_{a,H}
\simeq
\begin{bmatrix}
g\,\delta\theta_y\\-g\,\delta\theta_x
\end{bmatrix}
+\vct b_{a,H}+\vct n_H .}
\label{eq:riccati-axis-tilt-leak}
\end{equation}
The vertical channel has no corresponding first-order term, and horizontal bias
is less observable without an external kinematic reference.  The magnitude of this coupling depends on the vessel response, attitude
solution and sensor realization; it must be measured for the current source.

These ratios do not by themselves determine separate horizontal
pseudo-measurement covariances.  The scalar spectral reduction of
Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-regularizer-transfer} identifies the mechanism by
which $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$, $R_S$, and update cadence set a correction corner, but
it omits attitude-error coupling, axis-dependent bias observability, and the
directional desired-motion spectrum.  In particular, horizontal desired motion
is a heading-dependent projection of the same orbital field, so a fixed world
$x/y$ multiplier would not be invariant to vessel heading.  The supported
full-filter result is therefore narrower, but it is not isotropic. The pinned
vessel records are generated from an estimated fin-keel sailboat response, and
that hull resists sway while leaving surge comparatively free. A fixed
$\pm30^\circ$ heading projection would put the same horizontal RMS ratio
$\cos30^\circ/\sin30^\circ=1.732$ in every record; the records instead run
from $2.33$ at $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ down to $1.61$ at $H_s=\SI{8.5}{m}$, falling
with wavelength and approaching the geometric value only in the longest waves.
A heading projection cannot vary with period, so the anisotropy is a hull
response rather than a property of the record geometry, and every record holds
yaw at zero, so the world horizontal axes coincide with surge and sway here.
The deployed factors are accordingly $\rho_x=0.72$ on the free axis and
$\rho_y=0.50$ on the keel-damped one, with the vertical factor one. Pooled
over four fresh IMU draws this lowers mean pitch by \SI{17}{\percent} and the
lateral accelerometer-bias error by \SI{21}{\percent} at a cost of
\SI{4}{\percent} in roll, leaving yaw and both vertical channels unchanged. An
isotropic sweep to $0.5$ remains rejected on the same draws: it costs
\SI{5}{\percent} of yaw, which the anisotropic split does not. Because the
keel's anisotropy is a body-frame property while $r_S$ is applied in the world
frame, the two coincide only while the vessel holds the heading these records
hold; rotating the split by the estimated heading is the deployment-general
form and is not claimed here, since records that pin yaw cannot measure the
rotation. Further horizontal improvement should target the
disturbance itself---tilt error, accelerometer-bias observability, GNSS, or
another horizontal reference.  No fixed horizontal-to-vertical noise-intensity ratio is inferred from
the incident wave spectrum.

