\subsection{Bias tracking}

Figure~\ref{fig:bias-tracking} compares the estimated accelerometer and
gyroscope biases with their injected trajectories in the representative
medium-JONSWAP record. The bias states share the measurement residuals with
attitude and wave acceleration, so agreement with the injected bias is a
separate diagnostic from displacement accuracy. In particular, a smooth bias
estimate need not identify the physical sensor bias: persistent model error
can also be absorbed into these states.

For this sea, the ten-seed OU--III Adaptive ensemble gives a 3-D
accelerometer-bias RMS error of $0.0632\pm0.0330$~\si{m.s^{-2}} and a 3-D
gyroscope-bias RMS error of $(1.19\pm0.16)\times10^{-4}$~\si{rad.s^{-1}},
reported as mean and standard deviation over the final \SI{900}{s}.

The accelerometer comparison deliberately tests a mismatch between the
random-walk truth and the estimator's slow, temperature-centered
Gauss--Markov prior. Temperature is fixed, so these traces assess residual-bias
tracking rather than thermal calibration. The noise-free ablation in
Sec.~\ref{sec:model-mismatch-ablation} further shows nonzero estimated bias
with zero true bias, demonstrating that the bias error includes a
model-mismatch contribution. The representative trajectories therefore
complement the reconstruction scores without establishing bias identifiability
for arbitrary marine motion.

